  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1124 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nRidging over the CWA continues to dominate the pattern aloft. The \nother notable feature is a deep low pressure system moving eastward \nand now is places over Nevada. Prior to the system moving closer to \nthe forecast area, the weather will be calm with warmer than normal \ntemperatures this time of the year in the upper 70s and into the 80s \naccompanied by a breezy south wind at around 20 mph and higher \ngusts. An early round of weak precipitation is possible for the \nwestern counties at around a 10-20% chance. The bulk of the system \nwill impact the CWA into Saturday. \n\nTrends that were evident yesterday had the system moving slower and \nlater. That has remained into today's model runs. As a result, the \nbulk of the precipitation, especially the heavy and training rain, \nwill remain in Colorado on Saturday. By Saturday evening, some of \nthis rain is forecast to push into the far western counties of \nKansas. A second round of lighter rain is forecast to press further \ninto Kansas on Sunday morning. Because of the heaviest rain being in \nColorado, and the Kansas rain being spread out over a 48 hour \nperiod, a Flood Watch is not being issued at this time. Despite \nthat, with the current QPF amounts through the event reaching 2-2.5\" \nin the highest areas localized areas may see flooding. This is \nespecially true in urban and flood prone areas. Of the forecast \narea, the Hamilton county vicinity is expected to be the highest \namounts. The biggest concern is that the trend reverses and the \nsystem/precipitation is shifted 50 miles eastward leaving the \ntraining precipitation and gradient of heavy rainfall blanketing \nacross the western six counties and causing widespread issues. As a \nresult, monitor for future updates and potential flooding \nheadlines/warnings. East of highway 83, amounts will mostly be much \nlower at under an inch with some areas receiving little to no \nrainfall. The rain with lack the instability poses virtually zero \nsevere threat (only up to 600 J/kg in forecast soundings).\n\nBeyond this event, temperatures will cool dramatically closer to more \nseasonal temperatures for late October with highs in the mid to \nupper 60s. Midweek next week is forecast to be quiet with no active \nweather. The next chance for precipitation is on Friday, but with \nthe system still being poorly resolved this far out, time will be \nneeded for more specifics.",
